            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The moon primarily symbolizes the Church reflecting the light of Christ, with secondary meanings including cosmic judgment, appointed times, Israel, Mary, or a symbol of permanence.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The moon symbolizes the 'faithful witness' or the reflecting, submissive side of any relationship, applicable to the Son to the Father, the wife to the husband, and the Church to Christ.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The Church reflecting the light of Christ.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The reflecting, submissive side of a relationship.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_BROADENS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus limits the reflection metaphor primarily to the Church (or the individual believer), whereas the book abstracts the principle to apply broadly to the Son's submission to the Father and a wife's submission to her husband.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book affirms the consensus view (the Church reflecting Christ) but treats it as one instance of a broader relational pattern.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book explicitly acknowledges and includes the consensus view of the Church reflecting Christ's light, but broadens the core concept into a universal principle of submissive reflection, thereby adding the Son and wives to the symbol's extension.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The application of the moon to the Son relies heavily on an associative word-link ('faithful witness') between Psalm 89:37 and Revelation 1:5, which may equivocate on the phrase's contextual meaning across the two very different passages.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
